-- title:
--   Local path data at one scale: admissible points, revealing edges, and the block assembly
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d$, a configuration $\Gamma$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$, a scale step $\Delta > 0$, a radius parameter $R$, a logarithmic scale $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and a centre $z$. This module packages the data that produce, at that one scale and centre, a walk in the lattice graph $G$ for every pair of points the scale is responsible for.
--
--   **Admissible points and pairs.** A point $x$ is *admissible* at $(\Delta, m, z)$ when it is a lattice point with
--
--   $$\big\|x - \Delta^{m+1} z\big\| \;\le\; 2\sqrt{d}\,\Delta^{m+1},$$
--
--   which is exactly the one-point half of the admissibility condition on a pair; the left and right components of an admissible pair are shown to be admissible points.
--
--   **What an edge reveals.** Given $a \in \mathbb{N}$ and three maps $f, g, \mathrm{idx} : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z}$ — two hashes and an index function — an unoriented edge $e$ is said to *reveal* the pair $(x,y)$ when one of three things holds: $e$ has $x$ as an endpoint and the other endpoint $q$ satisfies $f(y) + g(x) = \mathrm{idx}(q)$ (the first jump); $e$ has $y$ as an endpoint and the other endpoint $q$ satisfies $g(x) = \mathrm{idx}(q)$ or $f(y) + g(x) = \mathrm{idx}(q)$ (the last jump); or $e$ has endpoints $p, q$ with $g(x) = \mathrm{idx}(q)$ and $f(y) + g(x) = \mathrm{idx}(p)$ (an interior edge). The counting result is that a revealing edge pins the two hash values down to boundedly many possibilities: if $P$ is any predicate on points and every fibre of $f$ and of $g$ over $P$ has at most $K_1$ elements, then for every edge $e$
--
--   $$\#\big\{(x,y)\ :\ P(x),\ P(y),\ e \text{ reveals } (x,y)\big\} \;\le\; 72\,K_1^{2},$$
--
--   because the edge confines $g(x)$ to one of eight values and $f(y)$ to one of nine, all computed from the edge alone.
--
--   **The bundled local data.** A `BlockData` at $(\Gamma, \Delta, R, a, K_1, N_0, m, z)$ consists of: a cone choice $W$; a family $\mathrm{Blk}$ of blocks in play and a subfamily $\mathrm{Jmp} \subseteq \mathrm{Blk}$ of blocks a point may jump into; representatives $\rho(B, i)$ for $i \in \mathbb{Z}/a\mathbb{Z}$ lying in the lattice and in the fibre of $B$ over $W(B)$; an index map recovering $i$ from $\rho(B,i)$; a walk of length at most $N_0$ through $\mathrm{Blk}$ joining any two blocks of $\mathrm{Jmp}$ in the choice graph; for every admissible point $x$ a block $B \in \mathrm{Jmp}$ all of whose points are joined to $x$ in $G$ by an edge of length at least $\Delta^m$; separation, that distinct blocks in play have all their points at distance at least $\Delta^m$; nearness, that every point of every block in play lies in the ball of radius $\Delta^{m+1}R$ about $\Delta^{m+1}z$; and two hashes $f, g$ each of whose fibres over the admissible points has at most $K_1$ elements. Assuming $\Delta > 0$, $R > 2\sqrt d$ and $a \ne 0$, this data assembles into the scale-level data with length bound $N_0 + 2$ and multiplicity bound $72K_1^2$: jump $x$ into a block, run the alternating lift of the block walk with labels $\alpha = f(y) + g(x)$ and $\beta = g(x)$, and jump out to $y$. Finally the module fixes the scheme's index count $\max\big(1, \Delta^{md}/L\big)$ for $L$ reference cones, floored at $1$ so that it stays positive at the bottom scale.
--
--   The choice of hashes is the load-bearing design decision: labelling with $f(y)+g(x)$ and $g(x)$ makes every consecutive index pair determine both hash values, and makes each of the two labels balanced in the variable the corresponding first or last jump ranges over, whatever the parity of the block walk's length. This is what turns a merely globally balanced assignment into one with a usable per-edge multiplicity bound.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Cardinalities are `Set.encard` values in $\mathbb{N}^{\infty}$, so the bounds are stated for possibly infinite sets; the index type is `ZMod a` and the assembly requires `NeZero a`. The parity case in the assembled walk is carried by an `if Even _ then _ else _`, so the endpoint of the lift genuinely depends on the block walk's length.
